ven by\n\nφ(x) = ((cot(πx)2+ 1,cot(πx)(cot(πx)2+ 1)) if x 6= 0,\n\ninjective on (0, π).\n\nNow we are ready to ﬁnish the proof of the theorem.\n\nProof of Theorem 3. As noted before, it is enough to construct a set S′⊂RP 2\n\nwith the two required properties, and take a projective transformation that\n\nmaps S′into R 2.\n\nFor the set S′we will take S′={φ(i/n) : i= 0,...,n−1}. Notice that by\n\nProposition 4 there are no four collinear points in S′. Indeed, if φ(x), φ(y), φ(z)\n\nand φ(w) were distinct and collinear, then z=−x−y=w in R/Z, giving a\n\ncontradiction.\n\nColour φ(i/n)\n\nred if 0 ≤i < n\n\n3,\n\ngreen if n\n\n3≤i < 2 n\n\n3,\n\nblue if 2 n\n\n3≤i < n.\n\n3\n\nLet l be a line that passes through two distinct points φ(i/n) and φ(j/n), 0 ≤\n\ni, j < n. Then either i+ 2 j≡0 (mod 3) or 2 i+j≡0 (mod 3), or l passes\n\nthrough a third point φ(k/n) where i+j+k≡0 (mod 3). In all cases it is not\n\npossible to choose i, j and k so that φ(i/n), φ(j/n) and φ(k/n) would have the\n\nsame colour and i 6=j. Therefore the line l is not monochromatic. This ﬁnishes\n\nthe proof.\n\nReferences\n\n[1] A.W. Hales and R.I. Jewett, Regularity and positional games, Trans. Amer.\n\nMath. Soc. 106 (1963), 222–229.\n\n[2] D. Husem¨oller, Elliptic curves (2nd ed., Springer, 2004).\n\n[3] T.S. Motzkin, Nonmixed connecting lines, Notices Amer. Math. Soc. 14\n\n(1967), 837.\n\n[4] A. P´or and D.R. Wood, On visibility and blockers, J. Computational Ge-\n\nometry 1 (2010), 29–40.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOpen Problem Garden (https://garden.irmacs.sfu.ca/comment/reply/59925)\nciteturn5search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.6 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; \"A counterexample to a geometric Hales-Jewett type conjecture\", J. ... The conjecture is a generalisation of the Geometric Hales-Jewett Theorem. ... Density Hales-Jewett and Moser numbers, arXiv:1002.0374, 2010.\n# Open Problem Garden\n\nImportance: Medium ✭✭\n\nAuthor(s):  